{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789486207,"nonce":"MiEWEi7YLZ76GwTjIpAgNYui9uA=","message":"Phase 1 shape, as built.\n\nTwo crates rather than one with two crate types: `ash_simd` (rlib) holds\nthe bodies as plain `extern \"C\"` functions and the primitive table\n(`prims.rs`, `for_each_prim!`), and `ash_hdll_simd` (cdylib) expands the\ntable into the DEFINE_PRIM resolvers of `simd.hdll`. One crate could not\ncarry both, because the runtime's `hlp_\u003cname\u003e` is the function itself and\nthe HDLL's `hlp_\u003cname\u003e` is the resolver: the same symbol with two meanings\ncannot be in one rlib that ash_std links.\n\nash resolves lib `simd` exactly as `std`: `std/src/simd.rs` expands the\nsame table into `#[no_mangle] hlp_\u003cname\u003e` wrappers, `build.rs` reads the\ntable into the static symbol table, and `native_lib::is_runtime_lib`\nreplaces the four `== \"std\"` tests (disk discovery, resolution, the AOT\nsymbol-vs-slot choice, the AOT shared-runtime decision). No hdll is looked\nfor, so a `simd.hdll` beside the program is never dlopened into ash.\n\nLane semantics chosen (the fixture pins them; phase 2 must match):\nwrapping integer arithmetic, shift counts masked to the lane width,\nIEEE 754-2019 minimum/maximum (NaN propagates, -0 \u003c +0), all-ones/zero\nmasks in the operand's lane width, bitwise select, in-order reductions\nwith integer sums wrapping in the lane width, saturating f32-\u003ei32 with\nNaN -\u003e 0. Reductions start from lane 0, so an LLVM `vector.reduce.fadd`\nmust be given -0.0 as its start value to agree.\n\nSignature strings follow hl.h: args, `_`, return -- `BiBiBi_v`. HashLink\nchecks them at load (`Invalid signature for function`), which the oracle\njob now exercises by building simd.hdll and running TestSimd on stock hl.\n(`ash_hdll_sqlite`'s strings do not follow this grammar; ash ignores them,\nstock hl never loads that crate.)\n\nParity cases gained a `classpath` field (single-line array, relative to\nthe tests directory) so a fixture can reach `haxelib/ash-simd`; threaded\nthrough the Rust harness, generate_parity_oracle.py, run_stdlib_matrix.sh\nand docker_compare.sh.\n\nValue types shipped now: Float32x4 and Int32x4 (the mask type). Their\nmethods are deliberately not `inline`, so they exist in the bytecode as\n`ash.simd.Float32x4.add` etc. for the phase 2 interception table. The\nother value types (Float64x2, Int16x8, Int8x16, UInt8x16) follow the same\npattern when needed.","files":null}]}